WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.300
Welcome to another special Friday episode of Office Ladies.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.800
This week, we are sharing our interview with Chelsea Fry from The Paper.

3
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.640
The Paper is coming out on September 4th on Peacock,

4
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.120
and we found out they are planning to release the first four episodes.

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.220
That's right.

6
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.360
We got our links to view them before they air.

7
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.340
Yes, they sent us the first four episodes.

8
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.000
I am so excited.

9
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.100
This is one of the benefits of being part of the Office universe.

10
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.380
We get to see these shows early, and, lady, I am here for it.

11
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.140
I know.

12
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.660
We get to see them, but we do have to wait to do our breakdown until after the show has premiered,

13
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:41.720
because no spoilers.

14
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.220
Yeah, so until then, we have to keep everything to ourselves.

15
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:53.600
Oh, by the way, Ange, did you know that there is a rumor that we have cameos on the paper?

16
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:54.720
Seriously?

17
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:55.620
Yes, yes.

18
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.680
Like, what kind of rumor?

19
00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:02.900
Okay, well, some website quoted you talking about how we went to the set,

20
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.120
and for some reason, that got turned into us having cameos on the show.

21
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.460
But you were just talking about how we got to visit.

22
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:11.820
Okay, I didn't muck it up, right?

23
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:12.740
I said visited.

24
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:13.880
No, no, no, no.

25
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:14.200
Okay.

26
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.260
You did not, lady.

27
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:15.540
Okay.

28
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.320
This is one of those examples of how things get twisted, the game of telephone.

29
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:20.820
Mm-hmm.

30
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
We should say, I guess, set the record straight, Angela and I do not appear in the paper.

31
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:27.520
Nope.

32
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.600
There is a familiar face on the paper.

33
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:30.480
That news is out.

34
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.740
Oscar Nunez is reprising his role of Oscar Martinez.

35
00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:34.220
That's right.

36
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.040
But Jenna and I are not on the paper.

37
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:36.460
Correct.

38
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:37.320
Are in the paper.

39
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.000
We're not on it or in it.

40
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:39.900
We are not.

41
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.020
We just visited it.

42
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:41.580
That's it.

43
00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.140
Well, I'm glad we cleared that up.

44
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.400
And now, we really hope that you enjoyed this interview with Chelsea Fry.

45
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.740
You might know her from the series, The Cleaning Lady or Animal Control.

46
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.620
She is an absolute sweetheart.

47
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.120
Chelsea shares about how she got her job on the paper and the secret code name everyone

48
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.860
had to use to keep the show under wraps on the Universal lot.

49
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:03.560
Mm-hmm.

50
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.320
We hope you enjoy.

51
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:09.900
All right.

52
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.700
Will you please tell us your name and tell us your character name?

53
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.180
I'm Chelsea Fry, and my character name is Mare Preeti.

54
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.700
And tell us a little about your character.

55
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:18.820
Sure.

56
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.720
Mare is, well, she's an Army vet.

57
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.060
She's born and raised in Ohio.

58
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.460
She's very practical.

59
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:38.140
I think she got really used to her dreams kind of like not working out.

60
00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:44.840
I think she, you know, worked here for a while and saw how it was run and didn't really think

61
00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.860
anything would change.

62
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:56.120
And then when Ned shows up, I think kind of this fire is reignited in her of hope and excitement

63
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.800
and doing something maybe she cares about with her life.

64
00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.500
Uh, I think she used to just like go to work as, you know, the job.

65
00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.220
And now it's kind of something more exciting, which is nice.

66
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.400
So catching her at an interesting moment in her life.

67
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.840
So you mentioned your character was born and raised in Ohio, but what about you?

68
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.280
I'm from Boston.

69
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.260
Um, I grew up in Andover, Massachusetts, small town, like right outside of Boston.

70
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.760
And then I moved to New York, um, for college.

71
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.420
So I was there like when I was 18, I lived there for six years and then I moved out here.

72
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.900
But now I got my entire family to move out here.

73
00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.460
So we're all California people now.

74
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:38.060
Yeah.

75
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:39.220
All right.

76
00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.600
So we always ask everyone, how did you get your job on the office?

77
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.560
So how did you get your job on the paper?

78
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.540
Can you tell us about your audition process?

79
00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:47.360
Oh my God.

80
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:48.460
It's crazy.

81
00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:56.720
I'm sure as you know, the stories always are, but I, um, I was in New York shooting something

82
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.480
and, um, I had a really rough year during the strike.

83
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:07.000
I, um, was the lead of a show for two years that was just a strike casualty.

84
00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.020
And they were just like, we're throwing it in the trash.

85
00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:16.680
And I was like, I just really did never, never saw it was kind of like my dream job that I

86
00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.860
was just like, well, that's, you know, now in the trash and nobody's ever going to see

87
00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:20.120
it.

88
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:26.160
And, um, I was in a dark, dark hole as I'm, you know, we all kind of worked during that

89
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:26.380
time.

90
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:33.980
Um, but anyway, I was luckily shooting a really sweet rom-com in New York, um, playing

91
00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.420
just a fun little side character.

92
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.960
And I did, you know, the audition, you'd like, didn't know anything about the part.

93
00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.500
And so it was, you know, kind of just like dummy sides.

94
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.460
So I was like, whatever, I'm just going to have fun with it.

95
00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.760
Like, I'm never going to hear about this again.

96
00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:51.320
You know, it was kind of like the rumor going around that it was like the office reboot,

97
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.300
but I have one thing to say.

98
00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:53.609
Okay.

99
00:04:53.609 --> 00:04:58.360
So we, so your audition sides were nothing that gave anything away about what

100
00:04:58.360 --> 00:04:58.880
you were doing.

101
00:04:59.120 --> 00:04:59.740
Is that correct?

102
00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.560
Yes.

103
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:01.300
Yes.

104
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:07.620
It literally said like untitled Greg Daniels and then the sides, I didn't even know what

105
00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:07.980
they were about.

106
00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.640
It was not about a newspaper.

107
00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.620
I didn't know what the character, who the character was, what literally nothing.

108
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.860
And so, um, yeah, I, I just kind of put it on tape and totally improvised it with my friend Blake.

109
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.500
And, um, he was so sweet and did it with me a bunch.

110
00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:26.400
And I was like, great.

111
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.360
Like, again, I'm never going to hear that.

112
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.380
Like, I'm just never going to actually be up for this job.

113
00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:40.240
Um, and so then I got a call that they wanted to see me, but I was in New York and, um, I,

114
00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.280
they were like, you can go to 30 rock, which was, I was like, okay.

115
00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.000
Like I interned there when I was in.

116
00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.200
I interned there.

117
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:47.560
You did?

118
00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.000
Yes.

119
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.420
I interned on Conan O'Brien.

120
00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.780
I interned at late night with Seth Meyers and then SNL.

121
00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.100
My like, I worked on the weekends on SNL.

122
00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.240
Are you kidding me?

123
00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.180
So it's like, so surreal.

124
00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:06.140
Like I like walked back in and, you know, met Michael Komen who was there and, um, we

125
00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:11.220
went in and it was like, I'm, I'm so, I'm so grateful that I thought I would never get

126
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.700
the job because I think I just had so much fun.

127
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:18.260
Cause I was like, I just, I'm never, again, like it just like, even to this day, I'm like,

128
00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.860
I'm never, I'm never going to get this job.

129
00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.080
Like I'm doing the job that I'm never actually going to get.

130
00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:29.380
And I went in and they kept me for like two hours and we just, they asked about me and

131
00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:33.560
about where I'm from and we're kind of like incorporating that into the character.

132
00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.640
And it was just one of the most surreal days of my life.

133
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.840
Like everybody else was on zoom besides Michael, but he was kind of looking at me, like giving

134
00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:43.780
me like a little thumbs up.

135
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.880
And, um, yeah, then I got a call later that day that I was, uh, in a chemistry read.

136
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.100
And this is where it's funny.

137
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:54.960
I thought the show was set in Boston, kind of like based on just like how we were talking.

138
00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.540
And so I started like making her really Boston-y when I came back in for the chemistry and they

139
00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:01.240
were like, what are you doing?

140
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.960
I was like, I thought she was supposed to have an accent.

141
00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.820
I was just kind of going crazy.

142
00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:13.040
And I then wrote, um, Greg, a letter saying that like, I just wanted the part so bad.

143
00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:15.220
And because it was dummy sides, I thought her name was Paula.

144
00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:17.360
I thought she was from Boston.

145
00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:23.160
I wrote this like heartfelt letter of why like nobody else can play Paula from Boston.

146
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.940
And it was like, I just really put it all out there.

147
00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.800
Cause I was like, when else am I going to get the chance to like write something to Greg

148
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:31.760
Daniels that he might actually read, like whatever.

149
00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:35.860
And then I got the part and her name's not Paula.

150
00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:37.080
She's not from Boston.

151
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.800
I was like so embarrassed.

152
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.940
I was like, oh my God.

153
00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:43.740
And I like made it seem like there's nobody else who can do this.

154
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:44.080
Like what?

155
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.520
I was terrible, but yeah, that was, um, and then I got the call and it was the craziest

156
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:49.640
thing ever.

157
00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:51.220
I still can't believe it.

158
00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:52.080
I'm like, yeah.

159
00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:06.710
So on the show, it's the same documentary crew that followed the folks at Dunder Mifflin.

160
00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.170
And now they're following you all here at the paper.

161
00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:15.370
What has it been like for you to like acknowledge the camera or look right at the camera?

162
00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:16.530
Was that a weird adjustment?

163
00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:18.750
Yeah, it is.

164
00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:24.750
Um, I think it's also, I mean, I cannot tell you how many times I've rewatched the office.

165
00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:30.930
It's like the thing that I, you know, it's as everyone in the world, it was like my comfort

166
00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:35.750
show, my everything show, the show that when I'm working and away from home, it like reminds

167
00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.210
me of home and makes me feel better.

168
00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.250
So I, I know everything.

169
00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.210
It was like one of the, he'd be like, you know, the episode and I would like pretend

170
00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:46.690
I'd be like, right, the, yeah, no, the, the dinner party episode.

171
00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.010
I think I've seen that one.

172
00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:49.530
I'm like, I've seen it a hundred times.

173
00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:57.010
Um, so anyway, I was so nervous about making it seem like I was kind of putting on something,

174
00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.690
like trying to do something that you guys had done.

175
00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.730
So the thing that I found was like trying to find like my exact relationship to it and

176
00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:11.730
like what, whatever, what I want from what I want whenever I'm looking at the camera and

177
00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:15.010
you know, what else do I want the camera not to know?

178
00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.570
Um, but it's definitely taken a minute.

179
00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:21.530
I feel like I actually don't know, but I feel like somebody was like, she needs to stop looking

180
00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.230
at the camera as much.

181
00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.010
Cause during a couple episodes, they were like, you don't need to look at the camera for that

182
00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:25.170
one.

183
00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:27.590
I was like, okay, I'm doing, doing it too much.

184
00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:31.430
Um, but yeah, it's been a fun, it's so fun.

185
00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:35.130
I've never done a show like that where you get to interact with it.

186
00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:39.070
I'm like scared for like the next thing, if I ever, you know, work again, the next thing

187
00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:40.450
I do, I'm going to like be like looking at the camera.

188
00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:41.310
They're going to be like, stop.

189
00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:42.510
It happens.

190
00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:42.750
Yeah.

191
00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.970
After the office, um, was a Netflix show.

192
00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:52.150
And, uh, I looked right down the barrel of the camera one time and they're like, Hey, Angela,

193
00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:54.230
can you, um, not look down the camera?

194
00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:56.570
That makes you feel better.

195
00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:00.330
Um, wow.

196
00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:01.170
I have so many.

197
00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:02.610
I want to ask you guys questions.

198
00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.050
Is it, how is it being back here?

199
00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:07.130
How do you guys feel?

200
00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:12.610
Well, it's really exciting to see some of our crew members that crossover.

201
00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.370
Um, that's been actually really emotional.

202
00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:22.450
Um, I mean, I wish all the time that I could wake up and just like go back to one day in

203
00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.790
the past and be on the set again.

204
00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:30.630
And, and just with all the people, including our crew and, um, make an episode.

205
00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.010
So it's been a little bit, I feel sentimental.

206
00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:34.750
Yeah.

207
00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:40.770
Well, we walked up and the first person we saw was Sergio from, you know, big time catering.

208
00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:44.330
He, I mean, Sergio fed us for nine years.

209
00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:51.210
He got us both through like pregnancy cravings and, uh, the cast and the crew that we're still

210
00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:51.850
like a family.

211
00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.790
I think of us as a family and we still support each other and show up for each other.

212
00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.670
So I hope that for you, cause it's really special.

213
00:10:59.590 --> 00:10:59.990
Yeah.

214
00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:05.610
It's, that has been one of the nicest things is hearing how many people came back from doing

215
00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:05.730
it.

216
00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.110
And they were like, it was, I mean, they talk about it all the time.

217
00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.210
They're like, cause, cause it's also true.

218
00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:16.650
Like now, you know, knock on wood, you get a show that goes on longer than a couple of

219
00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.110
seasons, but it's so rare.

220
00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:24.810
And to hear about like, you know, them seeing their children grow up and seeing you, like

221
00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:28.750
all of you just go through marriages and it's just, it's crazy.

222
00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:35.330
It's, it's, uh, so special and it's so fun to hear about with, um, yeah, every, I mean,

223
00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:39.550
I'm just like thinking of every, every single person on the crew, but it's, uh, it really

224
00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:45.950
feels like what you guys created has just truly carried on and made something so special

225
00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:47.310
and you can feel it.

226
00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:50.690
I feel so lucky to even just like be around it and hear the stories.

227
00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:55.850
Well, I know that you mentioned that your family is all out here with you now.

228
00:11:55.850 --> 00:12:02.210
And we also know that everyone has been sworn to secrecy during this whole taping.

229
00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:07.150
What was it like when your family found out you booked this job and what has it been like

230
00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:11.490
for you at holidays when they want to know stuff and you cannot tell them what, what is

231
00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:12.450
the situation?

232
00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:16.530
Like, do you have like an aunt who's like pestering everyone and you're on like lockdown information?

233
00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:21.150
Well, first could probably get fired for saying this literally like my agents called me when

234
00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.250
they told me they were like, do not tell anybody.

235
00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:25.110
I was like crying, I was like, Oh my God.

236
00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:30.670
I was also like on this, I was like at a dinner and, um, you cannot tell it, like, do not tell

237
